%S A086183 3,1,4,1,5,9,9,9,9,9,9,26,26,26,26,79,79,79,79,84,97,97,97,97,97,97,
%T A086183 97,97,97,97,97,97,592,592,592,592,592,592,592,592,592,592,592,592,
%U A086183 592,592,592,592,592,592,592,592,592,592,592,592,592,592,592,592,592
%N A086183 a(n) is the largest number appearing as a substring in both d(1)..d(n) and d(n)..d(2n-1), where d(k) is the k-th decimal digit of Pi.
%C A086183 1 <= A086184(n) <= n <= A086185(n) <= 2*n.
%C A086183 A000796(A086184(n)+j) = A000796(A086185(n)+j), 0 <= j < A055642(a(n)).
%C A086183 For record values see A086186, A086187.
%H A086183 Michael S. Branicky, <a href="/A086183/b086183.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A086183 Dave Andersen, <a href="http://www.angio.net/pi/piquery">The Pi-Search Page</a>.
%H A086183 Michael S. Branicky, <a href="/A086183/a086183.py.txt">Python program for OEIS A086183, A086184, A086185</a>.
%H A086183 Eric Weisstein's World of Mathematics, <a href="https://mathworld.wolfram.com/Pi.html">Pi</a>.
%F A086183 a(n) >= A000796(n) = d(n), since d(n) is in both in intervals. - _Michael S. Branicky_, Aug 03 2026
